WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
Elena wants a dependable benefit for a lifelong family obligation.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.400
Traditional whole life combines permanent death protection with scheduled

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.040
premiums and contractual cash values.

4
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.200
The guarantees depend on meeting the policy's requirements.

5
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:22.580
Compare the guaranteed schedule with any additional illustrated values, and

6
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.140
check the sustainable cost.

7
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.680
An early surrender may return much less than the premiums paid.

8
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.940
The word permanent describes the intended coverage pattern;

9
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.080
it does not excuse unpaid required premiums or erase loan obligations.

10
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.380
A permanent need can make whole life relevant, but the amount

11
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.340
and funding still need to fit the client's circumstances.

12
00:00:48.282 --> 00:00:53.362
An ordinary continuous-pay whole life design requires level

13
00:00:53.362 --> 00:00:59.342
scheduled premiums over the contract's stated long payment period, commonly

14
00:00:59.342 --> 00:01:02.742
through the insured's lifetime or a stated age.

15
00:01:03.762 --> 00:01:07.382
The premium can stay level even though the cost associated with

16
00:01:07.382 --> 00:01:12.722
aging does not. Early funding and the insurer's reserves support

17
00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:17.602
the long-term promise. Do not interpret a level premium as a

18
00:01:17.602 --> 00:01:21.502
statement that every dollar is placed in a personal savings account.

19
00:01:22.142 --> 00:01:25.122
Review the actual guaranteed cash-value schedule.

20
00:01:25.922 --> 00:01:31.082
Also read any stated maturity age and provision instead of assuming

21
00:01:31.082 --> 00:01:36.262
every policy ends or pays out at one universal age.

22
00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:40.796
Marcus buys a twenty-pay whole life policy at age thirty-five.

23
00:01:41.376 --> 00:01:44.076
He makes all twenty required annual payments.

24
00:01:44.936 --> 00:01:48.816
The scheduled premium obligation is then complete, while the whole life

25
00:01:48.816 --> 00:01:50.756
protection continues under the contract.

26
00:01:51.656 --> 00:01:53.896
This is not a twenty-year term policy.

27
00:01:54.796 --> 00:01:59.256
A paid-up-at-sixty-five design instead defines the payment period by the

28
00:01:59.256 --> 00:02:04.196
stated age. For otherwise comparable coverage, compressing premiums into

29
00:02:04.196 --> 00:02:07.156
fewer years generally requires a higher annual payment.

30
00:02:07.736 --> 00:02:11.016
Paid up means the required policy premiums have been satisfied.

31
00:02:12.156 --> 00:02:16.616
It does not mean the insured has died, the policy has

32
00:02:16.616 --> 00:02:20.756
been surrendered, or a death benefit has already been paid.

33
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.150
Elena compares another whole life design funded with one substantial premium

34
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.210
at issue. A single-premium whole life policy is a form of

35
00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:32.090
limited-payment coverage. Its required premium is paid once and it provides

36
00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.550
the protection and immediate cash values stated in the contract.

37
00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.950
Paying once does not mean the policy pays the beneficiary once

38
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.210
at issue. Nor does it make future loans or withdrawals free

39
00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.190
of consequences. Large early funding can affect federal

40
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.250
modified-endowment-contract treatment. That tax classification requires its

41
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.270
own test, which we will study later.

42
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:54.690
Keep the payment design separate from promises about access to money

43
00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:59.084
or tax results. A graded-premium whole life design starts with lower

44
00:02:59.084 --> 00:03:03.284
scheduled premiums and increases them during an initial period before they

45
00:03:03.284 --> 00:03:05.124
become level as the contract states.

46
00:03:06.044 --> 00:03:09.144
In a teaching schedule, the annual premium is three hundred dollars

47
00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:13.024
in year one, four hundred in year two, and five hundred

48
00:03:13.024 --> 00:03:15.804
in year three and afterward.

49
00:03:16.744 --> 00:03:19.744
The death benefit stays level in this example.

50
00:03:20.624 --> 00:03:23.984
A graded premium is different from a graded death benefit, which

51
00:03:23.984 --> 00:03:27.584
limits or changes the benefit during stated early years.

52
00:03:28.364 --> 00:03:29.544
Read what is being graded.

53
00:03:30.424 --> 00:03:33.864
Do not mistake an attractive first-year price for the continuing premium

54
00:03:33.864 --> 00:03:39.294
commitment. Suppose Marcus's in-force whole life policy

55
00:03:39.294 --> 00:03:44.494
states a one-hundred-thousand-dollar death benefit and has

56
00:03:44.494 --> 00:03:47.494
twenty thousand dollars of cash value.

57
00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:53.174
Assume no loans, added insurance, or other adjustments.

58
00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:59.794
The stated death benefit is one hundred thousand dollars, not

59
00:03:59.794 --> 00:04:02.254
automatically one hundred twenty thousand.

60
00:04:02.254 --> 00:04:07.714
Cash value is a living policy value governed by the contract.

61
00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:12.474
Surrender and a death claim are different events.

62
00:04:13.554 --> 00:04:18.734
Participating policies may provide dividends, but future dividends are not

63
00:04:18.734 --> 00:04:24.354
guaranteed. An illustration showing dividends covering premiums is

64
00:04:24.354 --> 00:04:28.134
different from a contractual limited-pay schedule.

65
00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:33.414
Read the guaranteed payment obligation before telling

66
00:04:33.414 --> 00:04:35.454
someone they can stop paying.
